[Music]  welcome to episode 9 of our series of  essary and devops I'm Liz Fung Jones a  site reliability engineer at Google  where I teach Google cloud customers how  to build and operate reliable services  and I'm Seth a developer advocate at  Google focused on infrastructure and  operations so so I was hoping to help me  figure out why are things so chaotic  every time one of my services break so  what kind of chaos are you experiencing  too much data slow internet connections  corporate firewalls VPNs no that's not  actually what I mean I'm talking about  human chaos there's often both too much  communication and too little  communication at the same time everyone  wants to participate people are doing  random things that don't actually help  and people actually doing the work  aren't talking ah yes humans are  definitely an important part of the  incident management process it sounds  like you need to define very clear roles  and responsibilities for each of your  humans so that they know exactly what  they can do without getting in the way  I'd love to figure that out how do I get  started Seth first you'll need a process  for declaring incidents a dashboard for  viewing your current incidents and some  kind of database for who to contact when  an incident is in progress for different  kinds of incidents you'll also need to  set thresholds for when to declare an  incident and who can declare an incident  in your organization depending on the  scale of the incident you may also need  to bring in specialists from other parts  of the company in order to help resolve  the incident right I wouldn't  necessarily want to declare an incident  every single time and get paged but I  also wanted to feel supported in  declaring an incident if I need to bring  in other people then what  so I mentioned having someone to contact  the most critical role in the incident  response process is that of incident  commander or IC who is responsible for  making strategic decisions and  delegating additional roles to other  responders the other roles can also be  played by the incident commander but  tend to be delegated as the incident  grows in size or complexity the first  role that tends to be created is that of  the operations lead who is in charge of  gaining a detailed understanding of and  actually changing the state of the  system like running commands grabbing  log files etc I guess that only having  one person touching the system makes a  lot of sense but I'm not sure how that's  helpful to have the incident commander  isolated from the details don't they  need to know all those details so yes  and no I mean have you ever been driving  a car and  the dark in some foreign country on the  opposite side of the road and you're  trying to get where you need to go but  you can't a few times yes and it's super  difficult like I usually have to pull  over because if there's no one else in  the car with me I can't both look at the  map and figure out where am I going  where am i trying to go and then I Drive  safely at the same time I can't do that  all at once right so I think you just  answered your own question right if we  apply that same analogy to the  operations world it's hard to both plan  and perform to keep track of all of the  fine-grained details at the same time so  it's usually best to separate the two  roles to minimize the overhead of  context switching I see that makes a lot  of sense so the incident commander and  the operations lead under them but you  mentioned multiple roles what other  roles might an incident commander want  to delegate well have you ever had  external stakeholders like a support  team or executives who are bugging you  for updates during an outage all the  time or maybe if you're a public-facing  company perhaps you have a presence on  social media where your users are  constantly nagging you about the service  being down oh it's super distracting  especially if I'm the Operations lead  but isn't that just an instant  commander's job to do sometimes up to  the point that the incident commander  can no longer effectively do both the  planning and the communications work at  that point you'll want a communications  lead for communicating with other teams  or external stakeholders I see and what  if an infant grows so large that the  decision-making responsibility and the  planning and the logistical work becomes  too much work for one person to handle  then what so in that scenario you might  want to have a dedicated planning lead  having someone be in charge of writing  the plan maintaining the running notes  document and starting to draft the  post-mortem another common role is a  logistics lead who handles things like  securing rooms making sure that people  have food and water and other supplies  or you could get more creative depending  on the needs of your organization and  your business that makes a lot of sense  what can't they in some command or  delegate though it sounds like the  insane commander can delegate anything  so the incident commander can't delegate  overall decision-making responsibilities  the buck stops with them they have to  help their subordinates make decisions  tiebreak and the event that people can't  agree and coordinate adding new people  into the Incident Response hierarchy as  deemed appropriate and what if there  need to be multiple people working hands  on keyboard or most people need to be  doing the communications how does that  work I thought you said only one person  is in charge of operations so additional  subject matter expert  can join under the operations lead or  the overall communications lead might  want to designate someone to be on the  customer support team while another  person is handling social media for  external communications and what about  the role of managers and technical leads  do I automatically become the incident  commander just because I'm the most  senior person who turned up no not  unless you're specifically asked to the  person who declared the incident is  always the incident commander until they  designate a successor it's up to them to  decide what roles in which to place  people joining or even turn people away  if there are too many cooks in the  kitchen for long-running incidents the  incident commander may delegate a  successor in a more appropriate time  zone or geography as an incident evolves  in size or complexity some incident  commanders may want to give up the role  of IC and instead become the operations  lead great that should help clear up  some of the confusion and chaos from my  next incident thanks everyone for  watching and we hope you'll tune in next  time to learn what to do after an  incident to find out when we post your  upcoming episodes please like and  subscribe below  [Music]

